<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sirmium</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sirmium was a Roman city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sava">Sava</a> river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a>, today Sremska Mitrovica in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>. In the late 3rd and 4th centuries it was one of the imperial residences of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tetrarchy">Tetrarchy</a> and the birthplace of several emperors, among them Probus. Later held by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrogoths">Ostrogoths</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gepids">Gepids</a>, it was taken by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a> in 582 after a long siege.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sirmium.jpg" alt="Sirmium" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: mediaportal.vojvodina.gov.rs · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carska_palata_Sirmijum1.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Sirmium stood on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sava">Sava</a> river, on the site of modern Sremska Mitrovica in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vojvodina">Vojvodina</a>, 55 kilometres west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>. Archaeologists have found traces of organised human life there from the fifth millennium BCE. The city was first mentioned in the 4th century BCE and was inhabited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyrians">Illyrians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celts">Celts</a>, among them the Amantini and the Scordisci. Its name means flowing water or wetland, from its position by the river, and the modern region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syrmia">Syrmia</a> takes its name from it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Roman proconsul Marcus Vinicius took Sirmium around 14 BCE, with the Scordisci as allies. It became a Roman colony in the 1st century CE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius">Marcus Aurelius</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius-gothicus">Claudius II</a> prepared campaigns there. In 103 it became the capital of Pannonia Inferior. With the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tetrarchy">Tetrarchy</a> of 293 it was one of the four capitals of the empire, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a>, Mediolanum and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicomedia">Nicomedia</a>, and the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galerius">Galerius</a>. From 318 to 379 it was capital of the prefecture of Illyricum. It had an imperial palace, a mint, a hippodrome, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatres</a>, baths and luxury villas, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ammianus-marcellinus">Ammianus Marcellinus</a> called it the glorious mother of cities.</p><p>Ten Roman emperors were born in or near the city, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius-gothicus">Claudius II</a>, Aurelian, Probus, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximian">Maximian</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantius-ii">Constantius II</a> and Gratian, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-i">Theodosius I</a> became emperor there in 378. Sirmium had a Christian community by the third century, and its first known <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>, Irenaeus, was martyred in 304. From 335 it was a stronghold of the Arian movement, and four synods on the Arian controversy met there between 347 and 358. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> conquered the city in 441, and later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrogoths">Ostrogoths</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gepids">Gepids</a> held it; the Pannonian Avars conquered and destroyed it in 582.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Remains of Sirmium lie beneath Sremska Mitrovica, and the site is protected as an archaeological Site of Exceptional Importance. Excavations have uncovered a palace at Glac, more than eighty altars from a sanctuary of Jupiter found in 1971, eight early Christian churches, and a hippodrome about 450 metres long under the town centre.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sirmium” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under Bayan I they besieged and took the Byzantine fortress of <b>Sirmium</b> in 582 after a long blockade, then captured Singidunum and raided deep into the Balkans.</blockquote><a href="/empire/avar-khaganate">Avar Khaganate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Byzantine army of Manuel I Komnenos, under Andronikos Kontostephanos, decisively defeated the Hungarians near <b>Sirmium</b>, forcing them to sue for peace on Byzantine terms.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-8">8 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>As he prepared to move against the usurper Magnentius, Constantius II needed a representative in the East and made Gallus caesar at <b>Sirmium</b>, giving him the name Constantius and his sister Constantina in marriage.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-15">15 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Gallus, appointed Caesar by his cousin Constantius II at <b>Sirmium</b> in March, reached his capital Antioch.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-7">7 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>They seized <b>Sirmium</b> (Sremska Mitrovica) on the Sava, a key city, and competed with the Ostrogoths, Byzantium and the Lombards for the middle Danube.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-the-gepids">Kingdom of the Gepids</a></li></ul></section>
